  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Ohio recorded $7.5B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date. The federal government issued 56,702 awards in the state at an average award size of $132K, and obligations fell 24.0% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in Ohio, obligating $4.9B so far this fiscal year. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences is the largest industry by obligations at $1.4B, followed by Petroleum Refineries at $636M and Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $555M. FY2026 to date obligations of $7.5B follow a sequence of annual totals: FY21 $8.4B, FY22 $7.8B, FY23 $10.1B, FY24 $9B, and FY25 $9.8B. Major vendors active in Ohio include GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY at $639M and MARATHON PETROLEUM COMPANY LP at $572M. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did federal obligations in Ohio change versus prior years?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 24.0% year over year in FY2026 to date. Ohio obligations for FY2026 to date are $7.5B, compared with FY25 $9.8B and FY23 $10.1B. The state saw a peak of $10.1B in FY23 and has trended lower through FY26 to date. The current-year total is partial because the fiscal year is still in progress."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate Ohio contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $4.9B in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration obligated $562M, the Department of Energy obligated $499M, and the Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $441M. GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY received $639M and MARATHON PETROLEUM COMPANY LP received $572M, with SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC at $377M and BATTELLE MEMORIAL INSTITUTE at $294M rounding out the top vendors."
      }
    ]
